---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import LifecycleNotice from "../../components/StandardLifecycleNotice.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/asbj/asbj10-revised2026.json";
---
<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code.replace(" (Revised 2026)", "")}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title.replace(" (Revised 2026)", "")}
  description={metadata.summary}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status="Revised 2026"
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "The Impetus for Reform — Convergence on Expected Credit Losses" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Central Change — From Incurred Loss to Forward-Looking ECL" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Staging Model & Loss Allowance Calculation" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Comparison — Original ASBJ 10 vs Revised 2026 Standard" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Worked Example — Staging Assessment for a Loan Portfolio" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Critical Judgments & Significant Increase in Credit Risk (SICR)" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Relationship to IFRS 9 Financial Instruments" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Related Standards & Implementation Guidance" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <LifecycleNotice
    type="effective-soon"
    title="Revised Standard (Effective 2026)"
    explanation="ASBJ released an exposure draft of proposed amendments to Statement No. 10 (Exposure Draft of Statement No. 89) on February 27, 2026, alongside Implementation Guidance on expected credit loss accounting for financial assets. This standard describes the confirmed revised framework."
    link="/asbj/asbj10"
    linkText="ASBJ Statement No. 10 — Accounting Standard for Financial Instruments (Base Standard)"
  />

  <div class="wrap">


  

<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A genuinely major step ending J-GAAP's long IAS-39-era stasis</div><div class="principle">As discussed in the companion ASBJ Statement No. 10 poster, J-GAAP's financial instruments impairment model has LONG remained conceptually CLOSER to the OLD, superseded IAS 39 incurred-loss approach than to IFRS 9's CURRENT expected credit loss (ECL) model. The 2026 exposure draft PROPOSES a GENUINELY MAJOR change: introducing an EXPECTED CREDIT LOSS model for financial ASSETS — the FIRST real step toward CLOSING this long-standing gap.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope of the Proposed Revision</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Impairment of financial assets, with companion guidance changes</div><div class="principle">The PROPOSED amendments apply to the IMPAIRMENT of financial ASSETS within scope of ASBJ Statement No. 10 — issued ALONGSIDE a DEDICATED new Implementation Guidance specifically on "Accounting for Expected Credit Losses on Financial Assets," plus CONSEQUENTIAL amendments to related fair value disclosure guidance and PRACTICAL/transferred guidance documents.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>THE HEADLINE CHANGE — Introducing an Expected Credit Loss Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Forward-looking loss estimation, replacing the old incurred-loss trigger</div><div class="text-supporting">The PROPOSED model REQUIRES recognising an ALLOWANCE for EXPECTED credit losses — estimated based on FORWARD-LOOKING information about FUTURE default risk — RATHER than waiting for a SPECIFIC, OBSERVABLE loss event (the OLD "incurred loss" trigger) before recognising IMPAIRMENT. This is CONCEPTUALLY the SAME fundamental shift IFRS 9 introduced INTERNATIONALLY when it REPLACED IAS 39 — recognising loss allowances EARLIER, based on EXPECTED future outcomes RATHER than confirmed PAST events.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Ending the Long-Standing Lag Behind IFRS 9</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Genuinely closing one of J-GAAP's most cited divergences</div><div class="text-supporting">This proposed revision represents the FIRST substantial step toward CLOSING the divergence FLAGGED throughout this poster series — J-GAAP's financial instruments standard REMAINING anchored to an "old IAS 39"-style APPROACH for an EXTENDED period AFTER IFRS itself moved on to IFRS 9. If FINALISED substantially as PROPOSED, this would represent a genuinely SIGNIFICANT convergence MILESTONE, alongside Revenue Recognition (No. 29), Fair Value Measurement (No. 30), and Leases (No. 34) as areas where J-GAAP has MOVED, or is MOVING, toward alignment with the CURRENT generation of international standards.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>What Likely Stays the Same — the Classification Categories</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A targeted impairment reform, not necessarily a full classification overhaul</div><div class="text-supporting">Based on the CONFIRMED scope of the exposure draft (impairment-focused, with a DEDICATED expected-credit-loss implementation guidance document), this revision APPEARS TARGETED primarily at the IMPAIRMENT/measurement-of-loss question, RATHER than necessarily a FULL replication of IFRS 9's ENTIRE classification architecture (business model and cash flow characteristics testing). The EXISTING four-category classification structure (trading, held-to-maturity, available-for-sale, subsidiary/affiliate investments — see the companion ASBJ Statement No. 10 poster) may WELL persist ALONGSIDE the new expected credit loss impairment approach, though this should be CONFIRMED against the finalised standard.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Companion Amendments — Related Party Disclosures and Revenue Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A broader, coordinated exposure draft package</div><div class="text-supporting">The SAME exposure draft package ALSO proposed amendments to ASBJ Statement No. 11 (Related Party Disclosures), ASBJ Statement No. 29 (Revenue Recognition), and ASBJ Statement No. 34 (Leases) — INDICATING this was a genuinely BROAD, coordinated standard-setting cycle addressing MULTIPLE related topics SIMULTANEOUSLY, rather than an ISOLATED financial instruments project.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — the Old Incurred-Loss Approach</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Waiting for a specific loss event before recognising impairment</div>
<div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example: A receivable under the pre-2026 model</div><div class="wk-body">A company holds a ¥100,000,000 trade receivable from a customer showing early signs of financial difficulty, but with no confirmed default event yet.</div><div class="wk-answer">Under the OLD incurred-loss approach, NO impairment is recognised UNTIL a SPECIFIC loss event (e.g. missed payment, formal distress signal) OCCURS — the receivable REMAINS at its full ¥100,000,000 carrying amount UNTIL such a TRIGGERING event is OBSERVED.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — the Proposed Expected Credit Loss Approach</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Recognising a forward-looking allowance earlier</div>
<div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example: The same receivable under the proposed model</div><div class="wk-body">Continuing the prior example, under the PROPOSED expected credit loss model, the company estimates a 5% probability-weighted expected loss on the SAME receivable, based on forward-looking information about the customer's deteriorating position.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company recognises an EXPECTED CREDIT LOSS allowance of ¥100,000,000 × 5% = <strong>¥5,000,000</strong> — EARLIER, and WITHOUT waiting for a CONFIRMED default event — REFLECTING the FORWARD-LOOKING nature of the proposed model, a MEANINGFUL change from the prior incurred-loss approach.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What the proposed amendments would require</div><div class="check-list"><span>Methodology and key assumptions used to estimate expected credit losses</span><span>Movement in the expected credit loss allowance during the period</span><span>Credit risk exposure information supporting the estimate</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether credit risk has increased significantly since initial recognition, if a staged approach parallel to IFRS 9 is adopted</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Forward-looking probability-weighted expected credit loss estimates</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS and US GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — J-GAAP (proposed) vs IFRS 9 and US GAAP's CECL model</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-1-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP (proposed, 2026)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS 9 / US GAAP (CECL, ASC 326)</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-1-1-1"><span>Impairment trigger</span><span>Expected (forward-looking) loss, proposed</span><span>Expected (forward-looking) loss — converged in principle</span></div>
<div class="tr alt cols-1-1-1"><span>Old incurred-loss model</span><span>Being REPLACED by this proposal</span><span>Already replaced (IFRS 9 in 2018; CECL under US GAAP from 2020)</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">If FINALISED as proposed, this would represent J-GAAP FINALLY catching up to a reform BOTH IFRS and US GAAP completed SEVERAL YEARS earlier — closing one of the MOST frequently cited J-GAAP divergence points discussed throughout this poster series.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every expected credit loss event would land</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Expected credit loss allowance</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span></span><span>Recognised earlier than under the old incurred-loss trigger</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 10 (original)</span><span>The base standard this Statement's exposure draft proposes to revise</span></div>
<div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 11 / 29 / 34</span><span>Companion standards amended within the same exposure draft package</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Expected credit loss (ECL)</div><div class="glossary-def">A forward-looking, probability-weighted estimate of credit losses, recognised before a specific loss event is confirmed, replacing the older incurred-loss trigger.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last"><div class="glossary-term">Incurred loss model (legacy approach)</div><div class="glossary-def">The prior J-GAAP approach recognising impairment only once a specific, observable loss event has occurred.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表) <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書) <span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部 <span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書) <span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</div></div>

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
